// Progression gates (docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md §5) — the content that decides
// WHEN a tier of the game becomes available, and what it makes available.
// Data, never hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron`,
// `machines.ron`, `shop.ron`, `ingredients.ron` and `contracts.ron`; the
// loader is `sim_core::sim::unlocks::UnlockRegistry`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way every other data file's loader does.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until it did, the game gated NOTHING: a new game
// could buy every ingredient and sign every contract in `contracts.ron`,
// so the only thing that grew as you played was the balance. That was fine
// while there was one sauce — there was nothing to hold back. BBQ is the
// second sauce, and handing a player the whole two-sauce board on their
// first minute makes the ketchup line look like a chore standing between
// them and the interesting one. So BBQ arrives, with an announcement, once
// the ketchup line has demonstrably run. Mustard, the third sauce, arrives
// the same way and later: it is a whole second line rather than a branch, so
// what its gate has to wait for is not only a player who has run the loop
// but a player who can PAY for another line of it.
//
// WHAT A GATE GATES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. An `Unlock` names
// two lists and only two: `ingredients`, the ids removed from the market
// (`data/ingredients.ron`) until it opens, and `contracts`, the ids removed
// from the contracts board (`data/contracts.ron`) until it opens. Both are
// enforced in the simulation — `BuyIngredient` and `SignContract` are
// REFUSED for a locked id, not merely hidden from a panel — so a gate is a
// rule rather than a decoration on the UI.
//
// **NO RECIPE IS EVER LOCKED, and there is no field here to lock one with.**
// The BBQ recipes sit in `data/recipes.ron` from a new game. They are simply
// unbuildable (their raws cannot be bought) and unsellable (nobody is
// asking). That is the whole reason this shape was chosen over an
// `unlocked: bool` on a recipe: a locked recipe would be a state every
// placed machine consults on every tick, on the hottest path in the
// simulation, to answer a question that changes at most a handful of times
// in a session. A gate that is a filter on two lists costs the tick nothing
// at all.
//
// WHY THE GROUP IS NAMED, RATHER THAN EACH ENTRY CARRYING ITS OWN
// `unlock_after`. The announcement is the point of the gate — a gate that
// opens silently is a gate the player never experiences — and an
// announcement needs a subject. "BBQ Sauce unlocked" is a sentence;
// "spice_mix unlocked, molasses unlocked, liquid_smoke unlocked, smokehouse
// trial unlocked, …" is a log. `name` is what the toast says, so it is
// player-facing prose and not an id.
//
// THE TRIGGER IS LIFETIME SUCCESSFUL CONTRACT DELIVERIES. Not money, not
// elapsed ticks, not machines placed. Money can be sat on, ticks pass while
// you read the keybinds, and a placed machine need never run — but a
// delivery is proof that a whole line ran end to end and something was
// shipped. It counts satisfied delivery windows only (`Economy::tick`'s
// `Paid` event); a missed window counts for nothing, and a lost contract
// does not take its past deliveries back. It is lifetime and never
// decreases, so a gate, once open, stays open.
//
// FIELDS, per unlock:
//   id                stable content id (the save format does not key on it
//                     — see below — but tests and the toast's diff do)
//   name              what the player reads in the announcement
//   after_deliveries  lifetime successful deliveries that open it; at least
//                     1, since 0 would be a gate that is open in a new game
//                     and therefore not a gate
//   ingredients       `data/ingredients.ron` ids held back until it opens
//   contracts         `data/contracts.ron` ids held back until it opens
//
// At least one of the two lists must be non-empty: an unlock that releases
// nothing is content that does nothing, and the loader rejects it rather
// than shipping a gate with no effect. No id may be gated by two unlocks.
//
// WHAT IS SAVED. The counter, and nothing else — `SaveData` stores
// `lifetime_deliveries` and derives every gate's state from it against THIS
// file. Saving "bbq_sauce is unlocked" instead would freeze a save against
// the numbers in this file on the day it was written; deriving it means a
// balance pass here re-reads correctly for every existing save.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    unlocks: [
        // THE FIRST GATE IN THE GAME (docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md §5, the BBQ
        // epic, 2026-09-18).
        //
        // TEN, and why. The starter contracts ask for 2-3 boxes every
        // 1200-2000 ticks, so ten satisfied windows is somewhere between
        // ninety seconds and three minutes of a line that works, depending
        // on how many orders the player has signed. That is long enough
        // that the ketchup chain has been built, fed, staffed and watched
        // through several delivery windows — the whole core loop, at least
        // once each — and short enough that it lands inside a first
        // session rather than being something a player reads about and
        // never sees. It is also a number the player can watch approach:
        // every docket on the clipboard counts its own deliveries.
        //
        // TEN EVEN THOUGH IT DOES NOT FIX ISSUE #55 — measured, not
        // argued, and re-measured after review (the balance pass,
        // 2026-09-20). #55 is that building the BBQ branch starves the
        // ketchup line while two new pipe runs and a vat prime, which is
        // several missed windows against three-strike contracts: the reward
        // for building the thing the game just unlocked is losing the order
        // that paid for it. The issue offers "let the tier arrive with
        // breathing room" as the cheap content-side fix — push this number up
        // until the player has slack by the time BBQ lands. So it was swept.
        //
        // THE PROTOCOL, because every number below moves if it changes.
        // Each run: build the shipped ketchup line through real purchase and
        // place commands; fund it; stock the larder with 1 200 of each raw;
        // leave hopper and tank auto-fill on; hire until fully staffed; sign
        // the starter contracts AT THE STATED MOMENT; wait for the stated
        // lifetime-delivery count; build the BBQ branch and its mixed exit;
        // then settle 15 000 ticks and read the board. "Bank" is finished
        // boxed_ketchup on hand at the moment the branch goes in. A gate
        // value other than ten is EMULATED by waiting for that many lifetime
        // deliveries before building — the shipped gate is long open by then
        // and nothing else reads the number, so it is exactly equivalent.
        //
        //   what the transient costs  boxed_ketchup output stops for about
        //                             8 500 ticks; roughly 35 boxes' worth of
        //                             throughput is absorbed into the new
        //                             pipes and vat before any comes back out
        //   1 order, gate 10          bank 22 — nothing lost
        //   3 orders signed AT THE
        //     GATE, gate 10           bank 22 — diner_supply lost
        //   3 orders signed at tick
        //     0, gate 10              bank 7 — corner_shop and diner_supply
        //                             lost to the branch
        //   emulated gate 20          bank 12 — 2 lost
        //   emulated gate 30          bank 19 — 1 lost
        //   emulated gate 40          bank 24 — NOTHING lost to the branch
        //
        // THE CONTROL MATTERS: with three orders signed at tick 0 and NO
        // branch ever built, market_stall still strikes out at tick 1 500.
        // That death is the ketchup line starting up, not the BBQ branch, and
        // it is why the "3 orders at tick 0" rows above count only what the
        // branch took.
        //
        // WHAT THE SWEEP ACTUALLY SHOWS. The deciding variable is the BANK AT
        // THE MOMENT OF BUILDING — not the number of concurrent orders, which
        // matters only through the bank it leaves. A delivery count steers
        // that bank by proxy and badly, because signing more contracts is both
        // how a player reaches the count sooner and what makes the transient
        // cost more. Pushed far enough the gate DOES work: forty deliveries
        // leaves the three-order player 24 banked and costs them nothing. But
        // forty is not "inside a first session", which is the entire reason
        // ten was chosen above, and a gate bought that way would be paying for
        // a transient with the thing the gate exists to deliver.
        //
        // Hence: TEN, kept because raising it to a value that would fix #55
        // costs more than #55 does. The trap stays open and documented rather
        // than papered over with a number, and #55 stays OPEN. The fixes it
        // wants are its own first option — say so at placement time, before it
        // costs anything, which the simulation has the information to do and
        // no surface to say it on (issue #92) — or a fluid network that will
        // not let a brand-new consumer take 100% of a network the old one is
        // living on (issue #93). Both are outside this file. Full record:
        // `docs/DECISIONS.md`, 2026-09-20, "The bank at the moment of building
        // decides whether the BBQ branch costs you the board".
        Unlock(
            id: "bbq_sauce",
            name: "BBQ Sauce",
            after_deliveries: 10,
            // The BBQ branch's three raws (`data/ingredients.ron`).
            // Finished ketchup is its fourth input and is NOT here: the
            // factory makes it, so it was never on the market to hold back.
            ingredients: ["spice_mix", "molasses", "liquid_smoke"],
            // Every contract that asks for BBQ, including `grill_pub` and
            // `roadhouse_group`, which ask for ketchup as well. A mixed
            // order is still a BBQ order — signing one before the branch
            // exists would be signing up to strike out.
            contracts: ["smokehouse_trial", "grill_pub", "roadhouse_group", "bbq_festival"],
        ),

        // THE SECOND GATE (the mustard epic, 2026-09-26), and it is holding
        // back a different KIND of thing from the first one.
        //
        // The BBQ gate holds back a branch: four recipes that hang off a line
        // the player has already built and paid for, so what it waits for is
        // understanding. This one holds back a whole second line from raw —
        // five machines, two hoppers, a tank, and the belt and pipe to join
        // them — so what it has to wait for is MONEY. A gate that opens onto
        // a tier the bank cannot afford is worse than no gate: it is an
        // announcement followed by nothing the player can act on.
        //
        // TWENTY-FIVE, MEASURED (the standard the BBQ gate's THE PROTOCOL
        // above sets). Same shape of protocol, one substitution: "bank" here
        // is the MONEY BALANCE, not boxed_ketchup on hand, because a mustard
        // line is bought rather than plumbed off an existing one.
        //
        // THE PROTOCOL. Build the shipped ketchup line through real purchase
        // and place commands (`sim::reference_chain_with_budget`, which prices
        // its own bill out of `data/shop.ron`); leave the remainder of
        // `sim::STARTING_MONEY` in the bank; hopper and tank auto-fill ON;
        // sign the stated orders at tick 0; then buy raws like a careful
        // player — top each one back up to the 20 units `data/storage.ron`
        // gives for free, and never spend below 30, because wages come first.
        // Read the balance at each lifetime-delivery mark. A gate value other
        // than 25 is EMULATED by reading the balance at that many deliveries:
        // nothing but this file reads the number, so it is exactly
        // equivalent.
        //
        // WHAT A MUSTARD LINE COSTS, out of `data/shop.ron`: 20 belt at 5,
        // 8 pipe at 8, 2 hopper at 40, 1 fluid_tank at 60, crusher 75,
        // mixer 100, cooking_vat 200, bottler 120, packer 90, dock 70 =
        // **959** — the same kit as the ketchup line, because it is the same
        // five stages in the same order. (A player who already owns a dock
        // and can reach it pays 889; the gate is sized against the whole
        // thing.) For contrast, the BBQ branch is 872 on top of a line that
        // already exists, and `STARTING_MONEY` leaves 2 041 after the first
        // line's 959.
        //
        //   1 order (corner_shop only,   10 deliveries  774
        //   as a new game ships)         25 deliveries  637
        //                                40 deliveries  489
        //   3 starter orders signed at   10 deliveries  868
        //   tick 0 (210 in signatures)   15 deliveries  1 002
        //                                20 deliveries  1 079
        //                                25 deliveries  1 216  <- the gate
        //                                30 deliveries  1 271
        //                                40 deliveries  1 475
        //
        // WHAT THAT SHOWS, and it is two different things.
        //
        // (1) For the player the board is built for — all three starter
        // orders signed, which `contracts.ron`'s balance note says is the
        // intended first several minutes — 25 deliveries leaves 1 216 against
        // a 959 line, arriving at about tick 20 000 (eleven minutes at
        // ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 30 ticks/sec). The line is affordable with
        // room for the mistakes, and it is affordable from about the 15th
        // delivery onwards; 25 buys a clear margin rather than a coin flip,
        // and still lands inside a first session, after the BBQ gate at ten
        // rather than beside it.
        //
        // (2) For the one-order player the balance FALLS, by roughly 10 per
        // delivery, and no gate value on this table ever reaches 959. That is
        // not the gate's problem and moving the gate cannot fix it: a factory
        // serving a single 2-box order overproduces into a delivery pool
        // nobody is buying from, which is exactly the loss
        // `contracts.ron`'s header describes, and the fix is signing the
        // other two starter orders (150 and 60, a window or two of payouts).
        // Raising the gate would make that player wait longer for a tier they
        // still could not afford, while making the intended player wait for
        // nothing.
        //
        // Growth past the gate is only about +13 per delivery (+27 per 1 000
        // ticks), so no nearby value materially changes affordability either
        // way — 20 would already do, 30 would cost a minute. The same control
        // the BBQ protocol records still holds: with three orders signed at
        // tick 0 and no second line ever built, `market_stall` strikes out
        // during start-up, so its death belongs to the ketchup line warming
        // up rather than to anything mustard does.
        //
        // WHAT IS NOT MEASURED HERE, honestly. These runs do not build the
        // mustard line and settle it, and they do not stack a BBQ branch's
        // 872 on top; a player who has just bought a branch is transiently
        // short, and the fix for that is the same one issue #55 wants
        // (#92/#93), not a different number in this file. The mustard line
        // has one advantage the branch does not, which is why it is not
        // another #55: it shares nothing with the ketchup line except
        // `vinegar`, so building it cannot starve a running line of anything
        // but bought stock.
        Unlock(
            id: "mustard_sauce",
            name: "Mustard",
            after_deliveries: 25,
            // Mustard's two raws (`data/ingredients.ron`). `vinegar` is NOT
            // here and must not be: the ketchup mixer drinks it from tick 0,
            // so gating it would lock the shipped line out of its own
            // ingredient. That is the one thing this gate cannot hold back,
            // and it is also the thing that makes the tier interesting.
            ingredients: ["mustard_seed", "turmeric"],
            // Every contract that asks for mustard, including `deli_chain`,
            // which asks for ketchup as well — a mixed order is still a
            // mustard order, and signing one without a mustard line is
            // signing up to strike out.
            contracts: ["deli_counter", "hot_dog_stand", "deli_chain", "sandwich_franchise"],
        ),
    ],
)
